CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD THE REGENT OF SOUTHEAST MINAHASA,

Considering:
a. that the State guarantees every person including women and children the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence and discrimination as guaranteed by the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that all forms of acts of violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights so that their dignity and honor need to be protected and their right to life guaranteed in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
c. that violence against women and children in the Region continues to increase, so that protection efforts are needed for women and children victims of violence through institutional support and Regional Regulations that guarantee protection for women and children victims of violence;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.

In View of:
1. Article 18 Paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times, most recently by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as has been amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
6. Law Number 9 of 2007 concerning the Establishment of Southeast Minahasa Regency in North Sulawesi Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 11, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4685);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 16 of 2011 concerning Legal Aid (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 104, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5248);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
10. Law Number 8 of 2016 concerning Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 69, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5871);
11. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6 of 2015 concerning the System for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
13. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 2036).

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SOUTHEAST MINAHASA REGENCY and THE REGENT OF SOUTHEAST MINAHASA Hereby Enacts: REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 2

The principles of Victim protection are:

a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims; and
e. empowerment.

CHAPTER II VIOLENCE

Article 5

Forms of violence against women and children include:

a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. domestic neglect;
e. exploitation; and
f. other violence.

CHAPTER III RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 12

Every Victim receives the following rights:

a. the right to have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of identity;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to obtain facilities in the judicial process; and
j. the right to assistance.

Article 13

Child victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 13, also receive special rights, as follows:

a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER IV OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Part One Regional Government

Article 14

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to implement efforts to protect victims of violence, in the form of:

a. establishing PPT;
b. establishing FPKK;
c. determining policies;
d. preparing program and activity planning;
e. providing facilities and infrastructure support; and
f. allocating budget.

2. In the implementation of the obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government prepares a Regional Action Plan for the protection of victims of violence.

Article 18

In addition to the obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 14, Article 15 and Article 16, the community has the following obligations and responsibilities:

a. preventing the occurrence of violence against women and children;
b. reporting when violence occurs;
c. protecting victims; and
d. providing emergency assistance;

Article 19

The family is obliged and responsible to implement prevention efforts and victim protection in the form of:

a. providing understanding about the prevention of domestic violence;
b. supervising every family member;
c. parents are prohibited from employing underage children in fulfilling the family economy; and
d. parents must provide opportunities for school-age children to obtain adequate education.

Article 21

In addition to prevention efforts carried out by the Regional Government as referred to in Article 20, prevention efforts must also be carried out by:

a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; and
d. other community institutions.

Article 24

The complaint service field as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter a has the task of:

a. conducting interviews and observation of the victim's condition;
b. making recommendations for follow-up services;
c. conducting coordination and referral to follow-up services and related parties; and
d. conducting administration of the complaint process.

Article 25

The health service field as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter b has the task of:

a. conducting examination, treatment, and follow-up care for victims;

b. conducting coordination of health rehabilitation implementation and medico-legal;
c. conducting supporting examinations and laboratory tests on evidence;
d. conducting consultation with specialist doctors or making referrals; and
e. making case reports.

Article 32

The provision of services to victims shall be implemented with:
a. quickly;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy to access;
f. free of charge; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 33

Forms of services to victims include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; and
f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 35

Assistance services as referred to in Article 33 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the medico-legal process;
c. accompanying victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
e. protecting the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;
f. coordinating with other assistants; and
g. providing continuous handling/assistance up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 38

Legal services as referred to in Article 33 letter e to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process by:
a. providing legal consultation that includes information regarding the rights of victims and the judicial process;
b. accompanying victims at the investigation, prosecution, and examination levels in court hearings and helping victims to fully describe the violence they experienced;
c. coordinating with fellow law enforcers, volunteer assistants, and social workers so that the judicial process proceeds as it should.

CHAPTER IX COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 48

1. The community shall participate in the provision of protection for Victims.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. establishing family partners at the sub-district/village level by the community;
b. establishing women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. independently conducting socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. providing first aid to victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
